{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:16.377Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399490 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399604 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186078499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399597 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399569 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399576 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399383 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186092236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399390 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186100985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399376 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399449 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399469 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186105863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399556 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399397 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124532 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399429 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124537 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399583 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124552 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399590 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124562 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124584 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399456 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399404 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399436 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122300 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5 \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124955 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n${x = 5; y = 4t; z = 7$\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124955 1074 "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122894 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399411 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $E$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}E = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125054 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125054 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122906 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399417 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125056 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-2$, $λ_{2} =-1$, and $λ_{3} = 1$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A = PDP^{-1}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125056 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122990 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399423 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 6xy + 2$ on the closed region $D = {(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 2}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125070 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6xy+2$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125070 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+6y & 2y+6x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=0 \\\\ 2y+6x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+6(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $10$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123158 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399443 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = B$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186125098 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186125098 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-8\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399543 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399483 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129125 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399476 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399523 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399530 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399516 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127231 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399463 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1071 "At which points on the surface $z = 3xy - \\ln y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v = ⟨3, 5,-1⟩$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129630 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨3,5,-1⟩?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129630 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3cy=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y}=5 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127315 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399504 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1071 "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t$, in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t$, where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129644 1071 "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129644 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The **displacement** $D$ of the skater **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127567 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399537 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129686 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is negative, which means that $P(0)<0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, only $P(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x-1$ satisties all these conditions." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127759 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399550 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129718 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129718 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 5\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=5$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =5+(-2)\\text{i}=5-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128011 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399563 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - v \\\\ u^{2} + v \\\\ e^{w}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186129760 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129760 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399617 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131893 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399611 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1071 "![](images/0e/0e05b575c22c78000a1ec235bb82cbfad3cd0cdfc6db370f19f094799b305949.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y = 2x^{2}$ and $y = 1 + x^{2}$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA =$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186134314 1071 "![](images/0e/0e05b575c22c78000a1ec235bb82cbfad3cd0cdfc6db370f19f094799b305949.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=1+{x}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186134314 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/da/da2895c09bc33f628626257b6d102e74875825df14ba744904e02ffa71552a02.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{1+{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187560 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186188919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399510 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1071 "The size of a bush growing in a schoolyard is being monitored. The area $A\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush at any given moment grows at a rate that's directly proportional to the area at that moment. Additionally, $2\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush is cut away every month.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the area of the schoolyard bush at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in months since it started growing?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186188919 1071 "The size of a bush growing in a schoolyard is being monitored. The area $Am^{2}$ of the bush at any given moment grows at a rate that's directly proportional to the area at that moment. Additionally, $2m^{2}$ of the bush is cut away every month.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the area of the schoolyard bush at time $t$, where $t$ is the time in months since it started growing?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186188919 1074 "The rate of change in the area of the bush per month increases in proportion to $A$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399497 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-35906" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "2c2420eb-a369-5201-ac95-195a1c7fe310" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $y=3$, we substitute $y=3$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-1 & =z+2 \\\\ z+3 & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at $(-1,-3).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1080 #uuid "5075bff5-d814-5717-a7ce-09c9a7f1c710" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1081 "$(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1084 #uuid "22da1385-6266-5799-b4e6-1450ac000f15" 13194139