7592186045585 true] [17592186399583 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399583 1072 #uuid "82160b99-37f5-52f2-85ae-e1d38b048f40" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399583 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399585 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399586 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399587 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399588 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399589 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399587 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399584 1080 #uuid "7901325e-ab13-5590-bad7-aa03b8ccdd1c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399585 1081 "$[\\begi